{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785185052,"nonce":"NCrE5VrAFSphLdPhEQ2aRaF2iI4=","title":"ADR-0062: scarab_workspace_warm_used_bytes now counts Snapshot Farms, and its help text lies","message":"Found while implementing s1 (`873d301`). `workspaced::run` gauges `scarab_workspace_warm_used_bytes` as `dir_size(warm_dir)`, and Snapshot Farms are built at `\u003cwarm_dir\u003e/farms` — **inside** it. So the gauge now includes Farms.\n\n**The location is not the bug and must not be \"fixed\" by moving it.** A Farm materialises by `reflink`, and a reflink only works within one filesystem, so Farms have to live on the same volume as `blobs/`. Keeping them under `warm_dir` is correct.\n\nThe bug is that the metric's help text says \"the warm content-addressed tier\", and a Farm built on the **copy** rung is duplicated bytes rather than CAS content — so the number silently changes meaning depending on which rung the filesystem took. Anyone reading this gauge to size a volume or tune eviction will draw the wrong conclusion, and on a reflink filesystem the duplication is nearly free while on ext4 it is not.\n\nDecide deliberately: either split into distinct gauges for CAS content vs Farms vs Exports, or keep one number and rewrite the help text to say exactly what it counts. Whichever way, eviction (`cba7165`) needs the breakdown, because a Farm and a blob are evictable under different rules — a Farm is rebuildable, a blob is the thing being cached.\n\n\n- [ ] The gauge (or gauges) state exactly what they count, and the help text matches on both rungs\n- [ ] Farms stay on the same filesystem as `blobs/` — a fix that breaks reflink is a regression\n- [ ] The breakdown is sufficient for `cba7165` to evict Farms and blobs under their own rules\n- [ ] Whatever is reported distinguishes reflinked (near-free) from copied (real) bytes, or says it cannot\n\n\n- 873d301 (s1) landing first","files":null}]}